{# Decision template: how much of a gossip someone is, read off their bio (ADR-001, "Gossip score").
   One request per named person, sent by Gossip.cpp the first time they matter and again whenever
   one of the bio blocks shown here changes, in the authored bio or a generated one. The DLL reads
   the answer to `gossip`: a score whose level comes back counted from 0, so 0 is the first line
   below and 4 the last. A bio that says nothing either way reads as about 1, and the DLL leaves
   the person's job score alone between 0.8 and 1.3 (tools/gossip_panel.py has the panel).

   Nothing earlier is shown: the same bio reads the same every time, and an earlier reading only
   drags the new one towards it.

   Context: gossip_npc (UUID, a JSON number), gossip_name.
   A criterion is exactly one line. #}
{% set _npc = decnpc(gossip_npc) %}
[ state person ]
{{ gossip_name }}, {{ _npc.gender }} {{ _npc.race }}.
{{ render_character_profile("bio_summary", gossip_npc) }}
{{ render_character_profile("bio_personality", gossip_npc) }}
{{ render_character_profile("bio_occupation", gossip_npc) }}
{{ render_character_profile("bio_skills", gossip_npc) }}
{{ render_character_profile("speech_style", gossip_npc) }}
[ end state ]

[ question gossip score ]
How readily does {{ gossip_name }} pass on news and gossip about other people to those around them? Judge by who they are (`person`): how fond of talk, how discreet, and what their work brings them to hear.
[ criteria ]
1: Tight-lipped: keeps what they know to themselves, and dislikes idle talk.
2: Discreet: says little about other people's business unless there's a reason to.
3: Ordinary: passes on news like anyone would, without going looking for it.
4: Talkative: enjoys the local talk and readily passes on what they hear.
5: Incorrigible gossip: collects every story going and tells it to anyone who will listen.
[ end criteria ]
[ end question ]
